### What is the Tasks Scheduling problem?

Given a number of tasks (numbered 0 to n-1) and a list of prerequisite pairs where [a, b] means task a must be completed before task b, determine whether it is possible to finish all tasks. This is the feasibility variant of topological sort.

```java
package com.bank.compliance;

import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.LinkedList;
import java.util.List;
import java.util.Queue;

public class TaskScheduling {

    public static boolean canFinishAll(int taskCount, int[][] prerequisites) {
        List<List<Integer>> adj = new ArrayList<>();
        int[] inDegree = new int[taskCount];

        for (int i = 0; i < taskCount; i++) adj.add(new ArrayList<>());

        for (int[] pre : prerequisites) {
            adj.get(pre[0]).add(pre[1]);
            inDegree[pre[1]]++;
        }

        Queue<Integer> queue = new LinkedList<>();
        for (int i = 0; i < taskCount; i++) {
            if (inDegree[i] == 0) queue.add(i);
        }

        int placed = 0;
        while (!queue.isEmpty()) {
            int task = queue.poll();
            placed++;
            for (int next : adj.get(task)) {
                if (--inDegree[next] == 0) {
                    queue.add(next);
                }
            }
        }

        return placed == taskCount;
    }
}
```

### Why does the placed count detect cycles?

If a cycle exists, the tasks inside the cycle never reach in-degree 0 because they are waiting on each other. They never enter the queue, so the placed count stays below the total task count. If no cycle exists, every task eventually reaches in-degree 0 and gets placed, so the count equals the total.

```java
package com.bank.compliance;

public class CycleDetectionDemo {

    public static void demonstrate() {
        // No cycle: 0->1, 0->2, 1->3
        // In-degrees: [0, 1, 1, 1]
        // Queue starts: [0]
        // Process 0: place 0, reduce 1 and 2 → queue: [1, 2]
        // Process 1: place 1, reduce 3 → queue: [2, 3]
        // Process 2: place 2 → queue: [3]
        // Process 3: place 3 → queue: []
        // placed=4 == taskCount=4 → true

        System.out.println(TaskScheduling.canFinishAll(4,
            new int[][]{{0,1},{0,2},{1,3}})); // true

        // Cycle: 0->1, 1->0
        // In-degrees: [1, 1]
        // Queue starts: [] (no task has in-degree 0!)
        // placed=0 != taskCount=2 → false

        System.out.println(TaskScheduling.canFinishAll(2,
            new int[][]{{0,1},{1,0}})); // false
    }
}
```

```text
true
false
```

### How does Kahn's algorithm work step by step?

Build the adjacency list and in-degree array from the prerequisite pairs. Enqueue every task with in-degree 0 (no prerequisites). Process the queue: for each removed task, increment the placed count and decrement the in-degree of all its dependents. If a dependent's in-degree reaches 0, enqueue it. When the queue is empty, compare the placed count to the total.

```java
package com.bank.compliance;

public class KahnTrace {

    public static void trace() {
        // Tasks: 0, 1, 2, 3
        // Prerequisites: 0->1, 0->2, 1->3, 2->3
        // In-degrees: [0, 1, 1, 2]

        // Queue: [0] (only task with in-degree 0)
        // Process 0: placed=1, reduce in-degree of 1 and 2
        //   In-degrees: [0, 0, 0, 2] → enqueue 1 and 2
        // Process 1: placed=2, reduce in-degree of 3
        //   In-degrees: [0, 0, 0, 1]
        // Process 2: placed=3, reduce in-degree of 3
        //   In-degrees: [0, 0, 0, 0] → enqueue 3
        // Process 3: placed=4
        // placed=4 == taskCount=4 → true
    }
}
```

### What is the time and space complexity?

| Aspect | Complexity | Notes |
|---|---|---|
| Time | O(V + E) | Each task and edge processed once |
| Space | O(V + E) | Adjacency list + in-degree array + queue |

### What are edge cases?

Edge cases include zero tasks (trivially true), tasks with no prerequisites (all can start immediately), a self-loop (task depends on itself, impossible), and disconnected components (each component is checked independently by Kahn's).

```java
package com.bank.compliance;

public class EdgeCases {

    public static void test() {
        // Zero tasks
        System.out.println(TaskScheduling.canFinishAll(0, new int[][]{})); // true

        // No prerequisites: all tasks independent
        System.out.println(TaskScheduling.canFinishAll(3, new int[][]{})); // true

        // Self-loop: task 0 depends on itself
        System.out.println(TaskScheduling.canFinishAll(1, new int[][]{{0,0}})); // false
    }
}
```

```text
true
true
false
```

### How is this different from the DFS cycle detection approach?

Kahn's algorithm (BFS-based) counts placed tasks and checks if all were reached. An alternative DFS-based approach marks nodes as visiting/visited and detects a back edge as a cycle. Both are O(V + E), but Kahn's is more intuitive for topological sort problems and naturally extends to the order-producing variant.

```java
// Kahn's (BFS): count placed tasks, check placed == total
// DFS: track visiting state, back edge = cycle
// Both: O(V + E) time, O(V + E) space
// Kahn's advantage: naturally produces the topological order
// DFS advantage: can also find strongly connected components
```

### What are common mistakes?

The most common mistake is forgetting to handle disconnected components. Kahn's algorithm handles this naturally because every component contributes tasks with in-degree 0 to the initial queue. Another mistake is modelling the edge direction wrong: [a, b] means a is a prerequisite of b, so the edge goes from a to b.

```java
package com.bank.compliance;

public class CommonMistakes {

    // WRONG: edge direction reversed
    // [a, b] means a before b, so edge a->b, not b->a
    // Reversing causes wrong in-degree counts and wrong results

    // WRONG: using adjacency matrix for sparse graphs
    // O(V^2) space instead of O(V + E)
    // Fix: use adjacency list
}
```

### How is this used in banking?

At the National Bank of India, the compliance team checks whether all regulatory tasks can be completed given their dependencies. If two tasks mutually depend on each other, the whole compliance workflow deadlocks and needs redesigning. Kahn's algorithm detects such cycles by comparing the placed count to the total task count.

```java
package com.bank.compliance;

public class ComplianceCheck {

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[][] prerequisites = {
            {0, 1}, // KYC verification before account opening
            {1, 2}, // Account opening before card issuance
            {0, 3}  // KYC verification before loan review
        };
        boolean canComplete = TaskScheduling.canFinishAll(4, prerequisites);
        System.out.println("All compliance tasks completable: " + canComplete);
    }
}
```

```text
All compliance tasks completable: true
```
